{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504429,"nonce":"sWrILqxPM7q/RspIJI8tnUOI+xU=","target":"353e4cc401cfd80fc4e5ed7e321d32788b0cf37cc4ee2a518b802df2e7634160","message":"**What is wrong.** `lintreach` treats `\u0026o.Alerts.Firing` as a write of `Firing` and a **read** of `Alerts`. Since `pgx` row scans are the tree's main way of filling a nested struct, any field whose only writer is a two-level `rows.Scan(\u0026x.A.B)` reads as never-written. Six such findings are sitting in `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt`, recorded as real debt, in a class the tool's own doc-comment claims cannot exist and in a file that explicitly forbids using it this way.\n\n**The demonstration.** The mechanism is two AST nodes, not one. `\u0026o.Alerts.Firing` is a `*ast.UnaryExpr` whose `X` is the outer selector `o.Alerts.Firing`; the walker marks that selector written at `tools/lintreach/main.go:643-648`. `record` then resolves `info.Selections[sel]`, whose `Recv()` is the type of `o.Alerts` and whose `Index()` is a **single** hop, so the write lands on `Firing`. The walk then descends to the inner selector `o.Alerts`, reaches `main.go:670-672` with `v.written[n]` false, and records a read. `Overview.Alerts` ends up with reads and zero writes.\n\nThe guard is `main.go:726`, and its comment names the assumption:\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\tlast := k == hops-1\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\t// Only the final hop can be a write; every hop before it is an\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\t// embedded field that had to be READ to reach the target.\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\tif write \u0026\u0026 last {\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\t\trec.writes[slot]++\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\t} else {\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\t\trec.reads[slot]++\n\u003e \t\t\t\t\t}\n\u003e ```\n\nThat is correct for a **promoted** selection — one selector, many hops through embedded fields — and wrong for a **chained** one, because by the time `record` sees a chain the AST has already split it into separate selectors with one hop each. The two shapes are indistinguishable at that point.\n\nThe doc-comment claims the class does not exist. `tools/lintreach/main.go:56-58`, verbatim:\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e // read of the embedded `args.Payload`. Those two — conversions and promotion —\n\u003e // were the ONLY false-positive classes the manual sweep hit, and both are handled\n\u003e // here rather than baselined.\n\u003e ```\n\nAnd the baseline forbids exactly what was then done with them. `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt:12-15`, verbatim:\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e # ⛔ Do not add a line to buy silence. If the thing is genuinely reachable by a\n\u003e # route the analyzer cannot see, use `//oto:reachable-ok \u003creason\u003e` on the\n\u003e # declaration instead; that is the mechanism for a FALSE POSITIVE. This file is\n\u003e # for a TRUE one you are not fixing today.\n\u003e ```\n\nThe six lines, and their real writers:\n\n- `baseline.txt:310-314` — `stats/domain.Overview.Alerts`, `.Channels`, `.Deliveries`, `.Groups`, `.Sources`, each reasoned *\"field is never written in production — nothing populates it\"*. All five are written by the single scan at `internal/stats/repository/stats.go:272-281`, which begins `\u0026o.Alerts.Firing, \u0026o.Alerts.Suppressed, …`.\n- `baseline.txt:276` — `notification/domain.Snapshot.Org`, reasoned *\"field is never written in production — written by 1 test site(s) and nothing else\"*. It is written by `internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go:274`: `Scan(\u0026snap.Org.ID, \u0026snap.Org.Slug, \u0026snap.Org.Name)`.\n\nThe clinching contrast is eight lines further down the same function. `internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go:282` does `g := \u0026snap.Group` — a **one-hop** address-of — and `Snapshot.Group` does not appear in the baseline at all. Same file, same repository, same scan, opposite verdicts; the only difference is whether the address-of crossed two field names.\n\nA seventh instance is latent rather than reported: `internal/sources/api/mapper.go:388` takes `\u0026r.PrometheusURL.Value`, and the containing field escapes a finding only because its struct is a decode target and `nowrite` is suppressed there.\n\n**Why it matters.** CONTEXT.md §5b makes the linters part of the validation contract, and lintreach exists because, as `main.go:5` puts it, oto's characteristic defect is code that *\"compiles, lints, type-checks, is exported, is schema-validated\"* and is wired to nothing. Its baseline — 288 entries — is the only inventory the repo keeps of that debt, and the file's premise, at `baseline.txt:9-10`, is that the list *\"can only shrink. A line here that stops matching is an error — the exemption cannot outlive the debt.\"* Salting it with six findings that are not debt breaks that premise in both directions: nobody re-reads a list they have learnt is partly noise, and the six lines will now be *removed* one day by someone who \"fixed\" a field that was never broken. The sibling gate shows what the target looks like — `tools/lintvocab/baseline.txt:12` records *\"The list is EMPTY, and that is the target state.\"*\n\nThe gate is also under-reporting. Any genuine `nowrite` on a struct-typed field that happens to be scanned two-deep is currently invisible for the same reason.\n\n**Where.** `tools/lintreach/main.go:56-58` (the claim), `:643-648` (address-of), `:670-672` (the inner-selector read), `:696-738` (`record`), `:726` (the guard); `tools/lintreach/baseline.txt:12-15` (the prohibition), `:276`, `:310-314` (the six lines); `internal/stats/repository/stats.go:272-281`; `internal/notification/repository/snapshot.go:274` and `:282`; `internal/sources/api/mapper.go:388`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** An address-of over a chained selector counts every field in the chain as a write, the six baseline lines fail as stale and are deleted, and `main.go:56-58` either names the third class as handled or stops claiming there were only two. A unit test over a fixture containing both `\u0026x.A` and `\u0026x.A.B` pins the distinction.","files":null}]}